# Installation guide for required Sphinx packages on Linux

pip install sphinx
pip install furo

# If you use other Sphinx extensions, add the corresponding pip install command here

---

# Project scripts and configuration

# Why use separate scripts for check and merge?
# ---------------------------------------------
# Keeping check and merge logic in separate scripts makes the project easier to maintain, debug, and extend.
# - You can add new checks or change the merge logic independently.
# - If a check fails, the build stops early, saving time and avoiding broken documentation.
# - This modular approach is best practice for teamwork and CI/CD pipelines.
# - Each script has a single responsibility, making it easier to test and understand.

- **merge_docs.py**: Reads metadata.json and automatically merges content from section files into the main chapter .rst files (between special markers). Run this script before building the documentation to ensure chapters are up to date.

- **metadata.json**: Defines the structure of chapters and their sections. Example:

  [
    {
      "chapter": "chapter1.rst",
      "dataList": [
        { "filePath": "chapter1/chapter1_section1.rst" },
        { "filePath": "chapter1/chapter1_section2.rst" }
      ]
    },
    ...
  ]

- **check_docs.py**: Checks all main chapter .rst files (as listed in metadata.json) for image directives (.. image:: ...) and verifies that the referenced image files exist. If any image is missing, the script will print an error and exit with a non-zero code (useful for CI or build scripts).

- **check_mermaid.py**: Checks all mermaid blocks in chapter and section .rst files for syntax that renders as "Syntax error in text" in the browser (leading edge arrows, dangling edges). It is integrated into the build pipeline — see "Run the scripts in this order" below.

Run the scripts in this order before building Sphinx HTML:
1. python merge_docs.py
2. python check_docs.py
3. python check_mermaid.py
4. sphinx-build -b html . _build/html

Note: `tel docs build` and `tel docs check` already run check_mermaid.py
automatically (build step 4/5, after merge + image check, before
sphinx-build). A mermaid syntax failure aborts the build, so the error is
caught at build time instead of showing up as a broken diagram in the
browser.

---

# Mermaid diagram rules (avoid "Syntax error in text")

Mermaid parses **each line as a separate statement**. An edge arrow
(`-->`, `---`, `==>`...) that starts on its own line is a syntax error and
the diagram renders as "Syntax error in text" in the browser (the Sphinx
build itself still succeeds, so the error is only visible at runtime).

**Wrong** — edge on its own line:

.. code-block:: rst

   .. mermaid::

      flowchart TB
          EVI["ContextInteractions"]
          -->|"onContextForAdjustmentChanged(contextId)"|
          II["InteractionsInfo"]

**Correct** — keep `node --> edge-label --> node` on ONE line:

.. code-block:: rst

   .. mermaid::

      flowchart TB
          EVI["ContextInteractions"]
          EVI -->|"onContextForAdjustmentChanged(contextId)"| II["InteractionsInfo"]

If a line gets too long, split the **label** with `<br/>` inside the
quotes — never split the statement across lines.

Checklist before committing docs with mermaid diagrams:

.. code-block:: bash

   # No edge arrow may start a line inside a mermaid block:
   grep -rn "^\s*-->" chapter*/ chapter*.rst
   # (must return nothing)

Also remember: `chapterN.rst` files are generated from
`chapterN/chapterN_sectionM.rst` by `merge_docs.py` — fix the syntax in
the **section files** first, then re-run the merge, otherwise the fix is
overwritten on the next build.